    "q-134029": {
      "dom_id": "step-q134029",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224903/assets/e2154f7bdd31e0c35517051fe46764ab75e597d0d78dbc64ffc55d1faecbec78.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224903/assets/687b257b9e2c6d3d2a015a5703d4fc29e9ab0598fb7e7ac69b588334c4a1e73d.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,4)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(6,6).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+b \\\\ 4 & =0.5(2)+b \\\\ 4 & =1+b \\\\ 3 & =b \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.5x+3.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
