    "q-134032": {
      "dom_id": "step-q134032",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224903/assets/d23d6a35343aebcb986118bd7c5bc720f00480706057ec900e55c75bac296ebb.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224903/assets/663e4d77d51816641a9fa861f37893d9f986d5e2b00e605ed5eb4fc19d40eab4.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(5,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(7,9).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-5}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=2$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(5,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x+b \\\\ 5 & =2(5)+b \\\\ 5 & =10+b \\\\ -5 & =b \\\\ b & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=2x-5.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
