      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224903/assets/e4b948bc417deab7c83cb40f53ad4b55fbfb169fc4ec761a9365856a9457a8f5.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224903/assets/fd3ce3de75fa9296c721e3f3a28bef241eae6350819c4cc7a54707921c0c1fe4.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,7)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(8,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-7}{8-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{6} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 7 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 7 & =-1+b \\\\ 8 & =b \\\\ b & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+8.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
